WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.856
Give the reason, not just the rule.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:05.677
A bare rule tells the model what to do in the cases you thought of.

3
00:00:05.957 --> 00:00:09.776
A rule with its reason lets the model handle the cases you didn’t.

4
00:00:10.056 --> 00:00:20.616
Anthropic’s docs make this point directly: explaining why an instruction matters gives the model more information about your goal, and it generalizes from the explanation.

5
00:00:20.896 --> 00:00:22.176
Compare two versions.

6
00:00:22.456 --> 00:00:23.949
“Never use abbreviations.

7
00:00:24.229 --> 00:00:40.400
” versus “These summaries are pasted into text messages to field technicians who read them on small screens, so spell out every abbreviation; a technician who misreads PM as ‘preventive maintenance’ instead of ‘project manager’ calls the wrong person.

8
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:49.640
” The second version also tells the model to keep things short, to prefer plain words, and that misreadings have consequences, none of which you had to list.

9
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.672
Context does the same work at a larger scale.

10
00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:57.965
Say what the output is for, who reads it, and what a good result looks like from their side.

11
00:00:58.245 --> 00:01:15.653
Anthropic’s real-world prompting course (adapted here) opens by contrasting weak and strong prompts for business tasks such as analyzing customer feedback, and the strong versions mostly add exactly this: who the output is for, what the categories mean, and what to produce.

12
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:25.789
When your own prompt fails, check first for information you took for granted, such as the audience, the definitions of your categories, or what to do with an unusual input.
